          type: "callout",
          tone: "interview",
          html: `<p>"Design a key-value store that stays writable during failures" is a classic prompt for this material. A strong answer states the quorum condition and why it works (two subsets of sizes w and r drawn from n replicas must intersect when w + r &gt; n), does the failure arithmetic out loud (n = 3, w = r = 2 tolerates one failure for both reads and writes), and names the machinery that keeps replicas converging: read repair for hot keys, Merkle-tree anti-entropy for cold ones, tombstones for deletes.</p>
<p>Then volunteer the caveat before the interviewer asks: quorums make stale reads unlikely, not impossible, and they do not give linearizability. A common slip is "a quorum is a majority of the cluster"; w and r are counted among the key's n replicas, and they need not be majorities, only satisfy w + r &gt; n.</p>`,
