          type: "text",
          html: `<p>The writes accepted by stand-in nodes are not forgotten. Each carries a hint naming the home node it was meant for, and once the interruption is over the stand-in forwards it there: <strong>hinted handoff</strong>. Until then the overlap argument has nothing to stand on, because the w acknowledgements and the r answers may come from different machines. A sloppy quorum is therefore an assurance of <strong>durability</strong> (the write is stored on w nodes somewhere), not of fresh reads. Calling it a quorum is generous: in the traditional sense, it isn't one.</p>
<p>Sloppy quorums are optional in all common Dynamo implementations. According to the book, Riak enables them by default, while Cassandra and Voldemort disable them by default. Cassandra still uses hints, but not the Dynamo way: its coordinator stores hints for replicas that are down (for up to <code>max_hint_window</code>, three hours by default), and those hints do not count toward the consistency level. The one exception is consistency level ANY, under which a write can be acknowledged when nothing but a hint has been stored.</p>
<p>These failure modes are not theoretical. Kyle Kingsbury's 2013 Jepsen tests partitioned Dynamo-style clusters while clients kept writing, and found that configurations resolving conflicts by last write wins silently discarded acknowledged writes, while Riak set up to keep siblings and merge them with CRDTs preserved them.</p>`,
