          type: "problem",
          html: `<p>Two clients, A and B, write key X at the same moment in a three-replica leaderless store. Network delays mean the replicas see the writes in different orders: node 1 gets A while B's message to it is lost, node 2 gets A then B, node 3 gets B then A. If each replica simply overwrites the value whenever a write arrives, nodes 1 and 3 settle on A, node 2 on B, and they never agree.</p>
<p>The intuitive fix is "keep the latest by timestamp". It has two flaws: for truly concurrent writes "latest" is meaningless, and keeping one silently discards the other, although both clients were told their write succeeded. Read repair and hinted handoff deliver writes in arbitrary orders too, so this is everyday behavior, not a corner case.</p>`,
