          type: "text",
          html: `<p>A side benefit: because the vector travels with the data, a client can read from one replica and write back through another without confusing the database about what it had seen. Riak 2.0 refined the scheme into <strong>dotted version vectors</strong>, which track causality more precisely and stop the number of siblings from growing without bound when many clients write concurrently.</p>
<p>Mind the terminology. The Dynamo paper calls its mechanism "vector clocks". Version vectors and vector clocks are closely related but not the same, and for comparing the states of replicas, version vectors are the right structure; <a href="#/ch9/learn/causality">Chapter 9</a> draws the distinction.</p>
<h3>CRDTs: merges that cannot go wrong</h3>
<p>A <strong>conflict-free replicated data type</strong> (CRDT) is designed so that replicas can accept updates independently and always merge to the same result. <em>State-based</em> CRDTs exchange whole states, and their merge function must be commutative, associative and idempotent, so states can be merged in any order and any number of times. <em>Operation-based</em> CRDTs exchange operations instead; concurrent operations must commute, and every operation must be delivered reliably and in causal order. The classic types:</p>`,
